.TH SVF 1 "2026-11-04" "Semantic Version Forge 1.3.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
svf \- automatic semantic version tracking and metadata synchronization
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B svf
.RI [ global-options ]
.I command
.RI [ command-options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B svf
manages a project's semantic version. It selects a canonical version source, calculates version increments from Git history, and synchronizes configured version metadata.
.PP
The Python distribution and package are named
.BR semver-forge ;
.B svf
is the installed command.
.PP
Normal output is intentionally concise. Use
.B --verbose
for expanded operation details and
.B --debug
for troubleshooting diagnostics and tracebacks.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B --project DIR
Operate on the specified project directory. Defaults to the current directory.
.TP
.B --json
Emit machine-readable JSON.
.TP
.B --verbose
Show expanded operation reports, including discovery results, synchronization details, and configuration scope information where supported.
.TP
.B --debug
Enable expanded diagnostic output and print Python tracebacks for caught operation errors. Debug mode includes verbose information.
.TP
.B --version
Print the installed Semantic Version Forge version.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B init [--yes]
Inspect project metadata and create
.I .semver-forge.toml
with the canonical version source and synchronization targets. Existing recognized version values are preserved. If recognized sources disagree, initialization stops and reports the conflict. If no usable version source exists, initialization creates a root
.I VERSION
file and uses it as the authority.
.IP
If configuration already exists, initialization asks before replacing it. Use
.B --yes
to authorize replacement without the prompt.
.TP
.B next [--dry-run] [--explain]
Inspect Git commits since the last processed commit boundary and select the highest configured semantic-version impact. Breaking changes select MAJOR, features select MINOR, and fixes/performance changes select PATCH by default. The selected version is written to the canonical source and configured synchronization targets when a bump is warranted. The processed Git boundary is saved so the same commits are not counted again.
.IP
.B --dry-run
previews the decision without writing.
.B --explain
includes the reason for the selected version or no-change decision.
.TP
.B sync [--dry-run] [--discover] [--version VERSION]
Write the canonical version to every configured synchronization target.
.B --dry-run
previews file changes without writing.
.B --discover
recursively searches for supported version metadata, adds newly discovered targets to the project
.I [sync].targets
configuration, and synchronizes them. The operation is idempotent: existing targets are not duplicated.
.B --version VERSION
uses the supplied version for this synchronization instead of reading the canonical version.
.TP
.B check [--verbose] [--debug]
Print the project name, canonical version, and synchronized file paths. The command is read-only. Verbose mode includes canonical-source validation, target values and statuses, discovered metadata, and configuration diagnostics.
.TP
.B config [OPTIONS] [get KEY | set KEY TOML_VALUE | unset KEY]
Inspect and manage Semantic Version Forge configuration.
.IP
With no action or selection, print the selected configuration file as stored, preserving its TOML syntax and comments. The default scope is the project configuration.
.IP
.B --list
prints recognized settings grouped by TOML table, with descriptions and defaults.
.IP
.B get KEY
prints one effective value and its source. The
.B --get KEY
form is also supported.
.IP
.B set KEY TOML_VALUE
sets a key in the selected scope. TOML values must use valid TOML syntax. Strings require quotes, booleans use
.B true
or
.BR false ,
and arrays use square brackets.
.IP
.B unset KEY
removes a key from the selected scope. The
.B --unset KEY
form is also supported.
.IP
.B --edit
opens the selected configuration file using
.B VISUAL
or
.B EDITOR
or falls back to
.BR vi .
.IP
.B --global
selects the user configuration file
.IR ~/.svfconfig .
.B --system
selects
.I /etc/svfconfig
or
.I $PREFIX/etc/svfconfig
when running in Termux.
.IP
.B --verbose
prints the configuration scopes, merged effective settings, precedence, and the source scope of each value.
.B --debug
adds diagnostic tracebacks when operations fail.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Project configuration is stored in
.I .semver-forge.toml
using TOML syntax. System and user configuration can supply shared defaults. For the TOML syntax, configuration schema, setting descriptions, data types, defaults, accepted values, and complete examples, see
.BR svf-config (5).
.PP
Use dotted paths with
.B config get
and
.BR config set ,
but use TOML tables and keys when editing the file itself. For example,
.B git.tag_prefix
refers to the
.B tag_prefix
key inside the
.B [git]
table.
.SH CONFIGURATION SCOPES
Configuration is merged in increasing precedence:
.IP 1. 4
System: /etc/svfconfig, or $PREFIX/etc/svfconfig in Termux.
.IP 2. 4
User: ~/.svfconfig.
.IP 3. 4
Project: .semver-forge.toml (preferred) or semver-forge.toml (legacy).
.PP
Higher-precedence values override lower-precedence values. Nested tables merge recursively. Arrays such as
.B sync.targets
are replaced by the higher-precedence scope, not concatenated.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
svf --help
svf --version
svf init
svf init --yes
svf check
svf check --verbose
svf check --debug
svf next --dry-run --explain
svf sync --dry-run
svf sync --discover
svf config
svf config --list
svf config get commits.types.feat
svf config set commits.types.feat '"minor"'
svf config unset git.baseline
svf config --global
svf config --system --edit
svf config --verbose
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .semver-forge.toml
Project configuration, canonical version source, synchronization targets, commit rules, and processed Git history boundary.
.TP
.I ~/.svfconfig
User configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/svfconfig
System configuration; in Termux use $PREFIX/etc/svfconfig.
.TP
.I VERSION
Default canonical version file for projects without an existing recognized authority.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success. A nonzero status indicates invalid configuration, unavailable project data, failed synchronization, or another operation error.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Python 3.10 or later. Git is required for commit-history-based version selection.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR svf-config (5),
.BR git (1),
.BR python3 (1)
